\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Synthesis.}
Image tokens remain instruction-blind; the readout is the sole multimodal convergence point.
Training shifts routing from mid-depth to entry-exit (L0--L1, L31), with effects at the readout ($R^2$ $+0.069$) rather than images ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$).
This instruction-blindness explains the visual dominance reported by Grant et al.~\cite{grant2026}: under RSA, DINOv2 achieves the highest geometric alignment ($\rho = 0.692$), yet VLA readout surpasses it in decodability ($R^2 = 0.678$ vs.\ $0.634$), pointing to instruction-conditioned integration.
Three independent analyses converge on L1 as the primary routing hub (\S\ref{sec:learned_routing}): attention patterns show training-dependent reorganization from mid-depth to entry-exit, representational probes show the largest ablation effect at L1 ($R^2$ $+0.042$ vs.\ L8 $+0.018$, L13 $\approx 0$), and action trajectory analysis shows the largest divergence ($d = 0.65$).

\paragraph{Mechanistic interpretation.}
Per-layer RSA bifurcates after L12 (Figure~\ref{fig:rsa_perlayer_curve}): readout $\rho$ declines gradually while image\_mean drops sharply ($\to 0.422$), consistent with mid-layer integration and deep-layer compression.
L1 ablation demonstrates causal influence on action output ($z = -2.73$ vs.\ random subsets) and is L1-confined ($|\Delta R^2| < 0.02$ at deeper layers).
The $R^2$ improvement reflects information gain in continuous action dimensions, where removing routing signals frees representational bandwidth; the gripper-concentrated trajectory effect ($72.3\%$ divergence) reflects sensitivity of discrete manipulation decisions. Both measure L1 routing's influence on different aspects of the action space.
The attention analysis reveals what the model learns (reorganized routing patterns), while ablation reveals how the function is implemented (distributed across the layer). Routing reorganization is the observable signature of L1's acquired collective role, not a property of individual heads.

\paragraph{Predictions.}
Other decoder-only VLAs (e.g., RT-2) should show similar entry-layer routing reorganization, given the shared constraint that the readout token integrates multimodal information under a causal mask.
Architectures with explicit cross-attention instruction routing (e.g., $\pi_0$) should show different specialization patterns.
L1 ablation on a LIBERO-fine-tuned model should produce measurable task performance degradation, particularly on instruction-discriminative tasks.

\paragraph{Limitations and scope.}
We study OpenVLA-7B on LIBERO. OpenVLA shares the dominant VLA design with RT-2~\cite{brohan2023_rt2} and $\pi_0$~\cite{black2024_pi0}; C1 follows from shared architectural constraints, but C2 may differ across backbone scales and decoder paradigms (Appendix~\ref{sec:ood_diagnostics},~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}).
Our analyses are representational and open-loop; connecting routing reorganization to task success requires closed-loop evaluation, which we leave to future work.

\paragraph{Use of Generative AI.}
Generative AI tools (Claude, GPT-4) assisted with code implementation and manuscript editing; all scientific content was produced by the authors.
